{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>You blew with Your wind.</b> Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 15:10) that it is from the root of נשף (twilight), for at twilight He brought a wind that submerged them. And Rashi explained (on Exodus 15:10) it as an expression of blowing, as in 'and He blows on them and they wither' (Isaiah 40:24). And he explained well. And it is further my opinion that its meaning is like with a בי״ת: נשבת ברוחך (You blew with Your wind), from the language of 'for the wind of YHWH blows upon it' (Isaiah 40:7), 'He makes His wind blow, the waters flow' (Psalms 147:18), for these two letters serve one meaning, like 'upon the wings of the height of the wall' (Proverbs 9:3) — על גבי (upon the back of), 'if he comes by himself' (Exodus 21:3), and likewise 'and property they shall plunder' (Daniel 11:24), 'You scattered peoples' (Psalms 68:31) — an expression of scattering. And so with names they interchange them: שובך (2 Samuel 10:16), שופך (1 Chronicles 19:16). And according to our Rabbis in the Mishnah (Pe'ah 6:1): הבקר לעניים הבקר (he proclaimed it ownerless to the poor), like הפקר (ownerless), and from 'the grains are shriveled' (Joel 1:17) they say עיפוש (Pesahim 45a), and they say בן פקועה (Hullin 69a) in place of בן בקועה, because it split open and they found there a nine-month-old fetus alive. And so in my opinion 'He has broken my teeth with gravel' (Lamentations 3:16) — הכבישני (He subdued me), from the language of 'and fill the earth and subdue it' (Genesis 1:28), 'He will subdue our iniquities' (Micah 7:19). And in their language there is also the term מידה כפושה (Yevamot 107a), like כבושה (subdued).\n\nAnd the meaning of the verse is that by Your wind — the strong and fierce one — the waters of the sea piled up, and You blew with Your wind by which You guide the sea, וכסמו ים, this is 'and the sea returned to its strength toward morning' (Exodus 14:27), for it returned to flow in it the wind as it does in its strength when its waves surge." "And the sense of <b>they sank like lead</b> is that they descended into the deeps like lead, and he mentioned this twice in the song — 'like a stone' (Exodus 15:5), 'like lead' — for this too was from them by the hand of HaShem, for there were among them many men who knew how to swim and who were close to dry land, and even the riders of the horses in the water were all likely to be saved, for horses swim a great deal upon the face of the water, and likewise those who held shields survive in the water, yet here not a single one of them escaped. And this is the sense of 'and HaShem shook off the Egyptians' (Exodus 14:27), for HaShem was lifting them up with His strong wind and casting them down into the sea, from the language of 'and the wicked are shaken out of it' (Job 38:13), 'and the strong shall be as tow' (Isaiah 1:31) — that which is scattered and cast off from the flax when it is prepared."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase וכסמו ים is textually difficult; translated hyper-literally as presented. It may be a corruption of a Hebrew phrase meaning 'its might, the sea,' paralleling לאיתנו in the cited verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:44.640288780Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}